{"rules":[{"jurisdiction":"NJ","level":"state","category":"security_deposits","status":"in_force","title":"New Jersey Security Deposit Law: deposit cap, annual increase limit, interest-bearing trust account","requirement":"A landlord is not required to collect a security deposit, but if one is collected it may not exceed one and one-half times one month's rent. Any yearly increase in the deposit may not exceed 10% of the current deposit. A pet security deposit is barred if, combined with the regular deposit, it exceeds 1.5 months' rent. Prepaid funds held to secure future rents (including last month's rent) count as part of the deposit, so the landlord may require no more than 2.5 months' rent at lease inception (1.5 months' deposit plus first month's rent). The deposit remains the tenant's property and is held in trust, not commingled. Landlords receiving deposits for fewer than 10 units must place them in an insured New Jersey bank or savings and loan account bearing interest at the current rate. Landlords with 10 or more units may instead use an insured money market fund of a New Jersey-based investment company or a variable-rate account. Interest earned belongs to the tenant. Where a tenant terminates under the Safe Housing Act, the landlord must make the deposit plus interest available within 15 business days after the lease ends, give written notice of pickup within 3 business days, and provide an itemized list of interest and deductions.","key_value":"Maximum 1.5x one month's rent; annual increase up to 10% of current deposit; interest belongs to tenant","coverage_conditions":"Most residential rental properties, including mobile homes.","exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings, although the tenant may make the law apply 30 days after sending the landlord a written request. The deposit-account/interest section does not apply to seasonal use or rental (term of not more than 125 consecutive days by a person with a permanent residence elsewhere); the landlord bears the burden of proving seasonal use.","coverage_logic":{"covers":null,"exempt":{"all":[{"field":"owner_occupied","op":"==","value":true},{"field":"units","op":"in","value":[2,3]}]}},"subject":"landlord","interaction":null,"yields_to_local":false,"preempts_local":false,"effective_date":null,"effective_rule":null,"enacted_date":null,"end_date":null,"penalty":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.2","quoted_span":"The maximum-security deposit to be collected by the landlord cannot be more than one and one-half times one month’s rent ( N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.2 ). It can be less. Any additional yearly security deposit increase may not exceed 10% of the current security deposit.","supporting_spans":["The exception is an owner-occupied two-, or three-family dwelling.","The interest or earnings paid on the security deposit belongs to the tenant and shall be paid"],"confidence":0.9,"conflict_note":null},{"jurisdiction":"NJ","level":"state","category":"just_cause_eviction","status":"in_force","title":"New Jersey Anti-Eviction Act: good cause required to refuse lease renewal","requirement":"A landlord of residential rental property may not fail to renew a lease, written or oral, unless it has good cause. A landlord cannot evict a tenant merely because the lease term has ended. The guide refers to a separate 'Eviction' section for the list of good causes (not included in this part of the document). A landlord may also refuse renewal for specified pet-related reasons in senior citizen housing projects.","key_value":"Good cause required to refuse lease renewal or evict","coverage_conditions":"Residential rental properties generally.","exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings (covered by separate provisions), motels or hotels, and transient or seasonal units.","coverage_logic":{"covers":null,"exempt":{"all":[{"field":"owner_occupied","op":"==","value":true},{"field":"units","op":"in","value":[2,3]}]}},"subject":"landlord","interaction":null,"yields_to_local":false,"preempts_local":false,"effective_date":null,"effective_rule":null,"enacted_date":null,"end_date":null,"penalty":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.3","quoted_span":"No landlord of residential rental properties except t hose in owner occupied two or three family dwellings, motels or hotels, transient, or seasonal units may fail to renew any lease, regardless of whether it is written or oral unless they have good cause not to renew the lease.","supporting_spans":["A landlord cannot evict a tenant just because the lease term has ended."],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_note":null},{"jurisdiction":"NJ","level":"state","category":"application_screening_fees","status":"in_force","title":"Mobile home parks: prohibition on entrance fees and required donations","requirement":"A mobile home park or private residential leasehold community landlord or operator may not require an entrance fee or other payment (including a donation or gift) from someone seeking to rent a space. Doing so makes the landlord or operator a disorderly person, and the payer may recover double the amount paid plus losses in Superior Court.","key_value":"No entrance fee or other payment to rent a space in a park/community","coverage_conditions":"Mobile home parks and private residential leasehold communities.","exemptions":null,"coverage_logic":{"covers":{"field":"property_type","op":"==","value":"mobile_home"},"exempt":null},"subject":"landlord","interaction":null,"yields_to_local":false,"preempts_local":false,"effective_date":null,"effective_rule":null,"enacted_date":null,"end_date":null,"penalty":"Disorderly person; payer may recover double the amount paid plus losses in Superior Court.","citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8C-2","quoted_span":"Any entrance fee or other payment required by the landlord to get into a park/community accepted by a landlord or operator makes the landlord or operator a disorderly person and may result in the person making the payment able to recover double the amount paid plus losses in Superior Court where the property is located.","supporting_spans":[],"confidence":0.5,"conflict_note":null}],"notes":"Part 1 of 4. The rent control/rent increase, credit and background check, and eviction causes sections fall in later parts (they are listed in the table of contents but their text is not in this part). The 15-business-day deposit return deadline appears only for Safe Housing Act terminations; the general return deadline may appear in the next part. Effective dates are not stated in this part."}
